WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.657
Express security often lives in the path
between request received and handler

2
00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:08.713
executed.

3
00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:16.059
Request handling depends on app-level
middleware, router-level middleware,

4
00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:19.717
route definitions, error middleware,

5
00:00:19.797 --> 00:00:24.243
and the order in which everything is
registered.

6
00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:30.287
Middleware can parse bodies, set security
headers, handle CORS,

7
00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:36.982
authenticate users, attach identity
context, enforce authorization,

8
00:00:37.062 --> 00:00:41.942
validate input, log requests, and
normalize errors.

9
00:00:42.022 --> 00:00:47.878
If the right middleware runs too late, or
only runs on one router,

10
00:00:47.958 --> 00:00:54.681
earlier routes may not receive the
protection the team assumes they have.

11
00:00:54.761 --> 00:01:01.918
A handler may look safe in isolation while
the request path around it is unsafe.

12
00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:09.589
For example, an admin handler might assume
authentication has already populated a

13
00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:17.477
user object, but the router could be
mounted without the authentication middleware.

14
00:01:17.557 --> 00:01:24.063
A webhook route may need raw body handling
for signature verification,

15
00:01:24.143 --> 00:01:29.348
while normal JSON parsing may be
appropriate elsewhere.

16
00:01:29.428 --> 00:01:36.694
A logging middleware may capture sensitive
values if it runs before redaction.

17
00:01:36.774 --> 00:01:43.063
Middleware order is not just style; it is
part of the security design.

18
00:01:43.143 --> 00:01:49.433
Trust boundaries exist between clients,
reverse proxies, Express,

19
00:01:49.513 --> 00:01:55.803
identity providers, databases, caches,
third-party APIs, queues,

20
00:01:55.883 --> 00:01:58.051
and internal services.

21
00:01:58.131 --> 00:02:05.180
Reviewers should trace how a request
enters the network, what the proxy adds,

22
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:12.309
what Express trusts, which middleware
runs, which identity is established,

23
00:02:12.389 --> 00:02:18.895
which authorization policy is checked,
which data stores are touched,

24
00:02:18.975 --> 00:02:21.578
and how the response leaves.

25
00:02:21.658 --> 00:02:29.140
That trace turns a loose collection of
routes into a reviewable application flow.
